<!-- Axol on the Jelly mobile base. GENERATED by scripts/build_jelly_urdfs.py
     from the Onshape export; do not edit by hand. With the parcel gripper
     (AxolConfig.gripper = parcel): a fixed jaw and a plate hinged on it, no
     wrist cameras. Arm links, joints and joint frames are the classic
     axol.urdf's, so FK and gravity compensation match it exactly; joint
     limits and meshes are the export's. Simulation model: rooted at the
     Jelly on the floor (link jelly, at the Jelly centre). Moving: the 14
     arm joints, the lift (lift, lift_2 mimics), each gripper's hinged plate
     (left_plate_joint / right_plate_joint, revolute over the export's half
     turn: left [-pi, 0], right [0, pi], 0 = folded flat along the jaw) and
     the four wheels (continuous). Arm joints carry AxolConfig's measured
     friction as <dynamics friction> (Coulomb, N·m) and damping (viscous,
     N·m·s/rad), per arm, and the motors' impedance gains as <drive
     stiffness> (kp, N·m/rad) and damping (kd, N·m·s/rad): a custom element
     no importer reads, so set them as each joint's position-drive gains
     after import. The x-drive's omni rollers are not modelled: plain wheels
     cannot strafe, so drive the base kinematically or with a holonomic
     controller. Arm masses and CoMs are AxolConfig's tuned gravity-comp
     values (the wrist-3 body is split so the jaw and plate carry their
     share while the assembly's total mass and CoM stay as tuned); every
     other mass is an ESTIMATE (SIM_MASSES in the generator) - replace with
     measured values. Inertia tensors assume uniform density over each
     part's convex hull. Effort/velocity: arm joints keep axol.urdf's
     values; the rest are estimates. Camera frames (*_optical) use the ROS
     optical convention (+z forward, +x right, +y down); an Isaac Sim camera
     attached with ROS camera axes needs no extra rotation. Mesh paths are
     relative to this file. Head camera frames (head_camera_*_optical, ZED X
     Mini Wide): each lens's projection centre from Stereolabs' model, 7.2
     mm behind its front cover.
-->
